From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Thu, 14 Apr 1994 11:38:36 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-107] SimAnarchy

Sasha, R. David Murray, Mark Grant, and Nancy Lebovitz write on SimAnarchy.

I think making an Anarchy simulation game would be *very* useful, not
only for promoting anarchy, but also to help us sympathizers clarify
our thinking on it.  But before you get big visions of building a game
to model a whole city, I think we should work out a very simple
"mininal" game model, and try to get some consensus that it is in fact
a model of the essense of the anarchy problem/situation we are
interested in.

A game needs an explicit set of possible states, payoffs to each
player as a function of the game state, and rules saying how the game
states depend on player choices.  My temptation would be to start with
a simple crime game, where folks can do some bad thing to each other,
and can hire protection agencies to do bad things in retaliation.
We'd have to be explicit about who can observe what, as well as who
can do what to whom.  There'd have to be some chance that there would
be witnesses to a crime.

If play in a minimal game like this could consistently evolve to some
sort of general peace, without the protection agences in frequent war,
and without one or a few big protection agencies taking over, I think
this would be a *major* contribution to the intellectual development
of anarchy.

If others are interested in this, I suggest folks think about and then
post proposals for minimal anarchy games, for the rest of us to
critique.  After a couple of iterations, we might really have something.

If we come up with a good enough yet simple game, I might be able to
direct some attention here at the Caltech social science department to
it.  "Attention" means working out some of the game theory of the
game, running some controlled experiments with real paid subjects, and
publishing the results.

Robin Hanson

