From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Date: Fri, 15 Apr 1994 00:49:15 -0500
Subject: [#94-4-110] SimAnarchy

At 11:38 AM 4/14/94 -0700, Robin Hanson wrote:
>If we come up with a good enough yet simple game, I might be able to
>direct some attention here at the Caltech social science department to
>it.  "Attention" means working out some of the game theory of the
>game, running some controlled experiments with real paid subjects, and
>publishing the results.
>
>Robin Hanson

Simulation versus formal analysis - I think these are separate issues, and
we'll be lucky to be able to combine them. A good sim type game should be
fun to play, very detailed and realistic. Such a game would be very
difficult to analyze. (I think your average MOO would be absolutely
intractable.) A good game theory approach should try to abstract from the
details, concentrating on the essential strategic interactions in a simple
(and probably boring to play) way.

I've no ideas on the realistic game approach. Axelrod's "Evolution of
Cooperation" and part of the literature inspired by that (including several
simulations involving genetic algorithms and similar techniques) provides a
first step toward the second goal. I think most of you have heard about
that. Axelrod held two public tournaments for strategies playing the
iterated prisoner's dilemma. He followed it up with an article in which he
1) used a GA to evolve winning responses to the strategies submitted to his
tournaments and 2) let the strategies play each other and evolve. Axelrod's
work can be interpreted as supporting the feasibility of
decentralized/anarchic/PPL type systems (under certain conditions).

We need to add in something that allows agents to recognize and remember
each other more, make agreements, and to organize on a small scale,
something that enables the PPL element.

Another spin we might put on this would be to make a bureaucracy game. Give
those budding socialists a shot at playing benevolent despot, and see if
they don't end up with famine in the Ukraine, peasant uprisings, factional
intrigue among the power elite, corruption, black markets, a
military-industrial complex burning pork, and institutional gridlock. Of
course, then it would be their turn to complain about the bias in the
parameter settings!

