From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 16 Apr 94 00:58:33 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-114] Pirie and The Wolf

John Styles writes:
>Does anyone know if the ASI was talking about covering Britain with
>forests in the technical sense (unfarmed land) or the traditional
>sense (big wood)? 

The newspaper article I have just mentions that sixty per cent of the
countryside would be wooded, adding that wild animals such as wolves and
bears would be reintroduced.  By "countryside" the ASI surely means rural
areas rather than total area, allowing room for some interpretation in
defining just how much local concrete is allowed before a region is no
longer "rural".

But 60% of rural areas converted to dense afforestation sounds far too
high to me, and I suspect they mean something more like nature reserves,
possibly becoming former nature reserves which allow hunting.  I'd support
some increase in wooded areas, but not too much.  A few minutes' walk from
where I grew up, the woods are only about 1.7 miles long by 700 yards at
their widest point (small by many people's standards, and with nothing more
ferocious than a few harmless flies which circle one's head in the summer).
Although still large enough an area to provide plenty of little tracks to
explore, it could do with being bigger.

With too much forest, though, you couldn't see the horizon for the trees.
I'd favour flatlands which were mostly open, and areas like Brecon Beacons
with great views from the higher parts, enabling you to look down onto some
wooded areas surrounding reservoirs and streams.  
 
>I think this is something a wide range of weirdos are in favour of
>in Britain for some reason - something to do with ideas of Merrie
>England or something. 

Demolishing houses to make way for trees would be a weird policy, and
unlikely to be supported by the market and the ASI.  I wouldn't mind if
some carnivores were reintroduced to selected areas.  After plenty of
publicity given to which areas were not approved for picnicking, and
measures to prevent migration of animals to "safe" zones, anything else
would be evolution in action.  Return of the carnivores needn't be a
retrograde step although in some ways would be a return to conditions of
centuries ago.  The common Brown Bear became extinct in England round
about the 11th century, and the last wild wolves in the British
countryside died out around the middle of the 17th century.

